# Sunfront Trooper — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Sunfront Trooper, an armored Duneforged infantry
soldier whose whole plated body deforms as one continuous skin — as a rigged 3D
character a game poses at runtime. There is no target model to copy: it must read
unmistakably as the Sunfront Trooper and satisfy the animation contract below.

This brief fixes what the trooper is and how it must move. It deliberately does not
give you a bone list, joint placements, or pose angles — working out the skeleton an
armored, marching, firing soldier needs, where the joints go, and how the skin binds to
it is the test. Invent the rig.

## How the tool works

`dc-skin` sculpts a **single, whole-body signed-distance field** — the entire trooper
at once — and Dual Contouring extracts it into **one continuous, sharp-edged surface**
fit for hard-surface armored plate. There is **no `--part` flag**: a skinned character
is one field with one log, and its skin deforms **across** its joints (an elbow bends
without a seam) rather than posing rigid segments about pivots. You build the model by:

- Sculpting the field with the CSG primitives — additive and subtractive `add-sphere` /
  `add-box` / `add-ellipsoid` / `add-cylinder` and their `subtract-*` counterparts,
  their soft `--blend` union, `replace-color`, `mirror`, and `clear` — each taking an
  opaque `#rrggbb` `--color`. Dual Contouring also carries a `--sharp`/`--smooth` tag
  for an edge. Primitive centers and extents are real-valued; the out-of-bounds portion
  is simply not meshed.
- Building a skeleton on top of the field: `define-bone` adds a bone under a parent (the
  first bone is the root), `set-bone` positions its head→tail, `define-joint` places a
  named degree of freedom on a bone, and `define-animation`/`add-keyframe` author the
  motion.

The **per-vertex skin weights are derived automatically** at `render` by bone-heat
diffusion (each vertex influenced by its nearest bones, capped at four) — you do not
paint them operation-by-operation. Where the automatic result is wrong for a region,
`paint-weight` overrides it (for example pinning a rigid helmet fully to the head bone
so it does not stretch).

Build one operation at a time. A sculpting or skeleton op only records — run `dc-skin
render` to (re)draw `model.png` and read it between calls, and run it before you finish
so the skinned `mesh.glb` geometry and `rig.json` are emitted (an unrendered model
scores as empty). `dc-skin render --time <ms> --animation <name>` renders the character
**posed with actual skin deformation** at that instant, so you can watch a joint fold.
`dc-skin --help` is the contract.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The field volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in field units,
  bounding the one whole-body field, which starts empty.
- **x** runs across the trooper, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. **y** runs up, `0` (ground, the boots) to
  `{{voxel.maxY}}` (top of the helmet). **z** runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`. **Forward is +z:** the
  trooper faces toward higher `z`, and the rifle points that way when shouldered.
- Build the trooper roughly symmetric left-to-right about the lengthwise vertical
  centerplane, tall and upright — a standing humanoid filling most of the height, with
  the torso and helmeted head stacked above the hips (the shouldered rifle deliberately
  breaks that symmetry).
- The whole body is sculpted as one field, in these shared coordinates, where it stands
  at rest.

## What the Sunfront Trooper is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the character must read unmistakably as all of these:

- An upright armored humanoid standing on two legs — a Duneforged foot-soldier in
  hard-surface plate, not a plain figure.
- A plated torso and a helmeted head with a clear amber visor.
- Two arms carrying a rifle that shoulders to fire (see the animations).
- Sand-bleached canvas wraps breaking up the bronze plate, and the palette below.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions, how the
plate is shaped and layered, how the helmet and rifle are formed, how the canvas is
draped, and how you break the body into a skeleton and place its joints. Nothing here
prescribes a shape; the test rewards a bold, characterful design that is unmistakably
the Sunfront Trooper and deforms convincingly.

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Armor plate — primary (bronze) | `#b07a35` |
| Armor plate — bright edges (gold) | `#d9a94e` |
| Deep plate, underside, shadow (dark bronze) | `#6b4a22` |
| Canvas wraps (sand-bleached) | `#d8c79a` |
| Canvas shadow (dun) | `#a8946a` |
| Rifle, boots, fittings (gunmetal) | `#565c64` |
| Deep recesses, shadow (dark iron) | `#31353b` |
| Visor glow (amber) | `#ff9d2e` |

Set a clear amber visor glow across the helmet so it reads from many angles.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with **three required animation declarations** by name (you
author the motion). Author each with `dc-skin define-animation` then `add-keyframe`,
choosing the period and each key's interpolation
(`--interp constant|linear|bezier|ease-in|ease-out|ease-in-out`, with optional
`--out-handle`/`--in-handle` for bezier) so a heavy, plated body slows into each
extreme and settles rather than sliding linearly. Every animation must read as **one
continuous skin folding across the joints** — the plate rides the deforming body; no
seam tears at a joint.

- **`march`** — the walk (a game-triggered playable that **loops**). The trooper
  strides forward in a purposeful two-phase gait: each foot plants and holds while the
  body passes over it, then lifts, swings forward, and plants again, the two legs in
  opposite phase, with the arms counter-swinging. Author it **in place** — the body
  stays centered in the volume and the clip must not translate the whole model across
  the scene; the leg cycle alone carries the stride, and a consuming game supplies the
  real forward travel. The skin folds across the hips, knees, and waist through the
  cycle.

- **`fire`** — the shoulder-rifle shot (a game-triggered playable that plays **once**,
  not looping). The trooper snaps the rifle up to the shoulder, braces, and takes a
  single shot whose recoil kicks back through the shoulder and torso and settles. The
  skin folds at the shoulder and elbow as the arm raises, and the recoil ripples through
  the torso as one continuous surface; the legs hold planted.

- **`brace`** — crouch behind cover (a game-triggered playable that plays **once and
  holds**, not looping). The trooper drops into a low crouch, knees bending deeply, hips
  and spine flexing forward and the head tucking down behind cover, then holds the
  crouched pose. The deep knee, hip, and waist bends read as the continuous skin folding
  across those joints, the plate compressing over it.

You may add extra bones, joints, and animations of your own (for example a subtle
breathing idle, or a gun that elevates); you must produce these three animations, by
these names, and must not contradict them (e.g. don't move the legs under `fire`, and
keep `march` in place).

## Working the tool

Sculpt the whole-body field with the CSG primitives, define your skeleton with
`define-bone`/`set-bone`, place joints with `define-joint`, and author the three
animations' keyframes — running `dc-skin render` and reading `model.png` (and posed
`--time`/`--animation` previews) between calls to confirm the character reads as the
trooper, the skin folds cleanly across the joints, and each animation reads with weight.
The per-vertex weights are derived automatically at render; `paint-weight` is there only
to fix a region the automatic result gets wrong. Run `dc-skin render` before you finish
so the skinned `mesh.glb` and `rig.json` are emitted. The recorded operation log and
`rig.json` are your submission.
